+---
+name: sprite-sheet-slicer
+description: Cut a grid sprite sheet (e.g. an 8x8 pet animation sheet) into fixed-size frames such as 128x128 without redrawing the art, and export a clean sheet, per-frame PNGs, animation JSON, a Godot SpriteFrames resource and a looping preview. Use when asked to slice, cut, split or animate a sprite sheet for Project P.
+---
+
+# Sprite sheet slicer
+
+Turns a generated sprite sheet (usually 8x8 cells, one animation per row or pair of rows) into game-ready frames. The art is **cropped and scaled only — never redrawn, repainted or regenerated**.
+
+## Inputs to collect
+
+1. The sheet image (attached file).
+2. Grid layout — default `8x8`.
+3. Output frame size — default `128` px.
+4. Row → animation mapping. The standard Project P pet layout is:
+
+   | Rows | Animation | `--anim` flag |
+   | --- | --- | --- |
+   | 1 | Egg idle | `egg_idle=1` |
+   | 2 | Egg hatch | `egg_hatch=2` |
+   | 3 | Pet emerging | `<pet>_emerge=3` |
+   | 4 | Pet idle | `<pet>_idle=4` |
+   | 5–6 | Jump and landing | `jump_land=5-6` |
+   | 7–8 | Ticklish and recovery | `ticklish_recovery=7-8` |
+
+   Rows read left to right, 1-based. If the user gives a different mapping, use theirs. If they give none and the sheet looks like the standard layout, use the table above and say so.
+5. Pet name (used as file prefix, e.g. `owl`, `turtle`).
+
+## Run
+
+```bash
+pip install pillow --break-system-packages   # if Pillow is missing
+python claude-skills/sprite-sheet-slicer/scripts/slice_spritesheet.py <sheet.png> \
+  --name owl --grid 8x8 --frame 128 \
+  --anim egg_idle=1 --anim egg_hatch=2 --anim owl_emerge=3 --anim owl_idle=4 \
+  --anim jump_land=5-6 --anim ticklish_recovery=7-8 \
+  --fps 10 --godot --res-dir res://assets/pets/owl --out out/owl
+```
+
+Options:
+
+- `--fps` playback speed written to the JSON, Godot resource and preview (default 10).
+- `--godot` also writes `<name>_spriteframes.tres` (Godot 4 `SpriteFrames`, one looping animation per `--anim`). `--res-dir` is the Godot folder the sheet PNG will be placed in.
+- `--clean-alpha N` zeroes pixels with alpha below N. Off by default so the art stays untouched; suggest `--clean-alpha 16` when faint red/yellow fringes from AI background removal show up in-game.
+- `--nearest` uses nearest-neighbour scaling for true pixel art; default is Lanczos (premultiplied alpha, so edges don't bleed).
+
+## Outputs
+
+| File | Contents |
+| --- | --- |
+| `<name>_spritesheet_<F>.png` | Clean sheet, `cols*F` x `rows*F`, same order as the source |
+| `<name>_spritesheet_<F>.json` | Frame size, grid, fps, animation name → frame indices |
+| `<name>_frames_<F>.zip` | Every frame as `<animation>_<col>.png` (two-row animations get `_a` / `_b`) |
+| `<name>_spriteframes.tres` | Godot SpriteFrames (with `--godot`) |
+| `<name>_preview.html` | Self-contained page looping each animation on a transparency checkerboard |
+
+Hand the user the sheet, JSON and zip (plus `.tres` when they use Godot) and show the preview. If a Design canvas is open, add one artboard per pet showing the full sheet and one looping player per animation, using the uploaded sheet as a CSS `steps(<cols>)` background animation.
+
+## Things to tell the user
+
+- The script prints the source cell size. If it isn't exactly the frame size (e.g. a 1254 px sheet → ~157 px cells), frames were **scaled** to fit — slightly softer, not redrawn. For pixel-exact frames, export the source at `cols * F` px (1024 px for 8x8 @ 128).
+- Mention any visible colored fringe and offer `--clean-alpha` rather than applying it silently.
+- Cell boundaries use `round(i * width / cols)`, so non-integer cell sizes are handled without drift.
